Q: How to right align fixed position div inside a div A: <p>Use negative margin to make the fixed element positionable:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div style='text-align: right;'&gt;
 &lt;div style='display: inline-block;
             position: fixed; width: 180px; margin-left: -180px;'&gt;
   &lt;!-- Your content --&gt;
 &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>text-align: right</code> and <code>display: inline-block</code> allows for variable width of the outer <code>div</code>.</p>
<p>Do not position the inner <code>div</code> by <code>top</code> / <code>left</code> / <code>bottom</code> / <code>right</code>.<br />
Thereby the inner <code>div</code> gets positioned via (negative) margin based on its natural position as if it was positioned using <code>position: absolute</code>.</p>

Q: Python subclass dircmp A: <p>The issue is that <code>methodmap</code> is built in the body of <code>filecmp.dircmp</code>, so its values are the plain function objects from that class, which bypasses normal attribute lookup entirely.</p>
<p>So the subclass needs its own <code>methodmap</code>. I rebuild the whole mapping from the subclass, which picks up any phase you override.</p>
<pre><code>import filecmp

class DirCmp(filecmp.dircmp):
    def phase3(self):
        print(&quot;subclass phase3 method&quot;)
        xx = filecmp.cmpfiles(self.left, self.right, self.common_files, shallow=False)
        self.same_files, self.diff_files, self.funny_files = xx

DirCmp.methodmap = {
    attr: getattr(DirCmp, func.__name__)
    for attr, func in DirCmp.methodmap.items()
}
</code></pre>

